Claims identified & fact-check:

Claim 1: A myth about ADHD is that people just need to try harder or are being lazy, when in fact they really are trying hard
→ Supported — ADHD is classified in ICD-11 as a neurodevelopmental disorder involving genuine impairment, not a motivational deficit; this directly counters the "laziness" myth, consistent with the disorder's classification and essential features describing symptoms as outside the person's typical control rather than effort-based

Claim 2: ADHD is real for adults
→ Supported — ICD-11 explicitly addresses ADHD in adults, including presentation, corroborating history requirements, and course features noting persistence into adulthood; NICE similarly has dedicated adult diagnosis and management sections

Claim 3: In adults, ADHD may show up as over-scheduling/over-committing to things without having the time
→ Unsupported — this specific manifestation is not listed in ICD-11's essential or additional features; the closest related concept (difficulty planning, managing tasks) doesn't specifically describe over-commitment to obligations

Claim 4: Losing track of time easily
→ Supported — matches ICD-11 inattention criteria: "having difficulty planning, managing and organizing schoolwork, tasks and other activities," which encompasses time-management difficulty

Claim 5: Forgetting to respond to texts or accumulating many unread messages because responding to everything feels overwhelming
→ Supported — matches ICD-11: "being forgetful in daily activities" and "having difficulty remembering to complete upcoming daily tasks or activities"

Claim 6: In relationships with a more structured/rigid partner, this can cause friction when plans aren't followed through due to lost track of time or organizational/planning difficulties
→ Supported (general) — consistent with ICD-11's additional clinical features, which state ADHD "can also strain interpersonal relationships across the lifespan, including those with family members, peers and romantic partners"

